  "query_preview": ".. Monsoonal moisture remains in place across the \nsouthwest as high pressure meanders over the four corners. Beneath \nthis ridge, PWATs in excess of 1\" continue to push north across most \nvalley locations this afternoon. Daily chances for showers, \nthunderstorms, and potentially flash flooding continue through at \nleast Friday across much of UT with this pattern in place, expanding \ninto southwest WY Tuesday and Wednesday. Deeper moisture looks to \nadvect further north starting Tuesday as some stronger synoptic \nforcing moves in from the west, increasing PoPs Tuesday afternoon \nbringing heavy rainfall and potentially flash flooding to all of UT \nand southwest WY.\n\nThunderstorm development across central and southern UT will occur \naround 1200-1400 this afternoon. PWAT anomalies across southern and \ncentral UT once again range from 0.5-1\" of water supporting a heavy \nrain threat. However, compared to other days, mid-level moisture has \ndecreased slightly as dry air has moved into the mid-levels. This \nwill serve to impact vertical development and overall intensity of \nstorms this afternoon. Regardless, instability in excess of 1000J/kg \nwith deep warm cloud layers up to 3km continue to support the \ndevelopment of efficient rainmakers, though more isolated this \nafternoon, with heavy rain expected from well established storms. \nOne failure mode that may occur today would be the increase in 500mb \nflow, assisting storms off the terrain which have otherwise remained \nanchored over the past few days. With faster moving storms, heavier \nrain rates may be needed in order to produce flash flooding, \nyielding some uncertainty with todays forecast given moisture \nquality is not as good as previous days keeping the flash flooding \nthreat more isolated. \n\nAs the ridge builds overhead, temperatures this afternoon are \nforecast to reach into the upper 90s to low 100s across most valley \nlocations across northwest UT. This has resulted in widespread \n\"major\" HeatRisk values, primarily within valley locations along the \nI-15 corridor and Great Salt Lake Desert, with a few areas reaching \n\"major\". As such, a heat advisory has been issued for the \naforementioned locations. \n\nOn Tuesday and Wednesday, moisture return increases with the mid-\nlevels moistening once again ahead of stronger synoptics further \nwest. Additionally, moisture is forecast to advect further north \nduring this time expanding the flash flooding threat to northern UT \non Tuesday, then to southwest WY on Wednesday. Enhanced flow aloft \nmay hinder the overall flooding threat, though anomalously deep \nmoisture (PWAT anomalies on the order of 0.5-1\") remain in place, \nsupporting potentially heavy rainmakers. Additionally, some shear \nwill be in place (0-6km shear on the order of 20-30kts) which will \nhelp storms sustain themselves rather than pulsing and gusting out, \npotentially enhancing the flash flood threat. Currently, the WPC has \noutlined much of UT on Tuesday and Wednesday in a Slight Risk (2/4) \nfor exceeding flash flood guidance, with southwest WY outlined in a \nSlight Risk on Wednesday. \n\nSome severe potential exists on both Tuesday Wednesday across \nnorthern UT and southwest WY as a few shortwaves traverse the Great \nBasin in the afternoon hours. With ample shear (0-6km 20-30kts) with \nCAPE from 500-800J/kg, a few longer-lived storms, perhaps a couple \ntransient supercells, may develop in the afternoon hours each day. \nConfidence regarding timing and location of these shortwaves remain \nhighly uncertain at this time, though the broad synoptic pattern \nassociated with this would support a potential damaging wind threat \nand potentially some small hail if it were to materialize.",
